<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">United States of the Ionian Islands</h1><p class="st-meta">1815 CE – 1864 CE · capital Corfu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">British protectorate over the seven Ionian Islands from 1815 to 1864, whose cession to Greece was the first enlargement of the Greek kingdom.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> fall, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1815">Treaty of Paris</a> in November 1815 made the Ionian Islands, formerly the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-ionian-islands">French Ionian Islands</a> and earlier the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/septinsular-republic">Septinsular Republic</a>, a nominally independent state under the exclusive protection of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corcyra">Corfu</a>, the last French-held island, had been handed over in 1814.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Real power lay with the British Lord High Commissioner. The first, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-maitland">Sir Thomas Maitland</a>, nicknamed King Tom, imposed the constitution of 1817, which left an island assembly and senate with little authority. The British built roads, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-academy">Ionian Academy</a>, but many islanders resented their rule. From the 1840s Radical politicians demanded union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and their assembly voted for it repeatedly.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> deposed King Otto, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> agreed to cede the islands to encourage the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-i-of-greece">King George I</a>. Under the Treaty of London the islands joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece</a> in 1864, and Corfu's fortifications were partly demolished.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/united-states-of-the-ionian-islands.jpg" alt="United States of the Ionian Islands" width="320" height="168"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_States_of_the_Ionian_Islands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1815 CE – 1864 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Corfu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Thomas Maitland, Henry Storks</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Italian, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ionian Islands, British Ionian Islands</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1840&amp;focus=united-states-of-the-ionian-islands" aria-label="United States of the Ionian Islands on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5172 759 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where United States of the Ionian Islands lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5492 1015L4997 651M5492 1015L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5492 1015h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5492 1015h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Corfu. <a href="/atlas?year=1840&amp;focus=united-states-of-the-ionian-islands">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
